\section{Unknown Is Not a Third Logic Voltage}

The Boolean calculus used to design a gate describes inputs drawn from \(\{0,1\}\). Robert G. Plantz explicitly works at this logic level in chapter~6 of \emph{Introduction to Computer Organization}, abstracting the electrical implementation. Verilog's simulator instead represents four logic values: \texttt{0}, \texttt{1}, \texttt{x} (unknown), and \texttt{z} (high impedance). An uninitialized register can start as \texttt{x}, a floating net can read \texttt{z}, and equally strong drivers that disagree can yield \texttt{x}. The simulator does not compute a voltage from these symbols. It also cannot use \texttt{x} to predict which value real hardware will read after a setup or hold violation.

The operators distinguish what can and cannot be determined. For example, \verb|1'b0 & 1'bx| is known zero, because either binary interpretation of the other operand gives zero. But \verb|1'b1 & 1'bx| is unknown. This is not full symbolic reasoning: \verb|a ^ a| is \texttt{x} when \texttt{a} is \texttt{x}, even though for every actual binary assignment to that one bit the mathematical result is zero. Some tools and gates can simplify the binary function despite that difference in simulation behavior.

\section{Put Two Drivers on a Net}

The following is Verilog-2005 \cite{ieee1364verilog}. This is a valid simulation model. Mapping it to a bidirectional pin depends on the device and tool; FPGA fabrics commonly do not provide arbitrary internal tri-state buses. Do not count this example as portable internal synthesis RTL.

\begin{verbatim}
module shared_pin (
    input  wire ea, eb,
    input  wire da, db,
    inout  tri  pad,
    output wire observed
);
    assign pad = ea ? da : 1'bz;
    assign pad = eb ? db : 1'bz;
    assign observed = pad;
endmodule
\end{verbatim}

Each \verb|assign| supplies an independent driver; neither assignment wins by appearing last. \texttt{tri} is a net type with ordinary wire resolution. If one enable is low, its driver contributes high impedance, so another driver can determine \texttt{pad}. If the two enabled drivers fight, the default equally strong drivers resolve to \texttt{x}. An external connection can add yet another driver, and explicit drive strengths can change the outcome. If an enable is \texttt{x}, a conditional expression on a one-bit result merges the two alternatives bitwise: driving a known \texttt{1} in one branch and \texttt{z} in the other produces \texttt{x}, not a guaranteed release.

% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Resolve First, Then Compare the Observation}

\begin{verbatim}
# Assume no external driver and equal default strengths.
for each pair (ea, eb) in [(0,0), (1,0), (0,1), (1,1)]:
    drive da=0, db=1 and set the two enables
    wait for all zero-delay propagation
    expect observed === next value from [Z, 0, 1, X]
# A matching X is a diagnostic, not a successful transfer.
\end{verbatim}

The \verb|===| in the sketch denotes Verilog's case equality: \verb|observed === 1'bx| produces a known Boolean answer, even if the operand is a meta-value. Ordinary \verb|==| may yield \texttt{x}. Use the former when the test is explicitly checking simulator symbols, not to claim that physical logic can detect an unknown voltage. \Cref{fig:visual-308} isolates the resolution operation from the processes that compute the driver values.

