VHDL-2008's IEEE \verb|std_logic_1164| package makes the distinction explicit through nine-valued \verb|std_ulogic| and its resolved subtype \verb|std_logic| \cite{ieee10762008}. The table has, among others, \verb|'U'| for uninitialized and weak values such as \verb|'H'|. Prefer unresolved types for internal signals that must have a single source, so an accidental additional driver is diagnosed instead of appearing as possible contention. When only one source is supposed to own a bus, also check ownership directly: two drivers supplying the same bit can produce a known output while still violating that protocol. A synthesis report that replaces an internal tri-state construction with mux logic does not repair that ownership error; it only chooses an implementation for the modeled resolution behavior.

\section{Separate Ownership From the Sampled Bit}

Suppose the receiver has a separate \texttt{transfer} indicator. The verification model now has three jobs: inspect the control bits, check that the bus is owned when the receiver samples, and compare against the owner's data. It cannot perform the middle check by looking at the resolved wire. In particular, the two cases that produce a known one in the table have different protocol outcomes. Conversely, an idle \texttt{Z} is not a fault unless the receiver claims to have accepted a word.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Contributions & Resolved bit & If transfer is asserted \\
\hline
Z, Z & Z & Missing owner \\
Z, 1 & 1 & Valid binary data \\
1, 1 & 1 & Multiple owners \\
0, 1 & X & Multiple owners \\
Z, X & X & Invalid data from sole owner \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{verbatim}
check_sample(transfer, ea, eb, da, db, pad):
    require binary(transfer) and binary(ea) and binary(eb)
    if transfer == 0:
        return                       # no payload observation
    owners := integer(ea) + integer(eb)
    require owners == 1
    expected := da if ea == 1 else db
    require binary(expected)
    require case_equal(pad, expected)
\end{verbatim}

Here \texttt{binary} accepts exactly zero and one. Check the controls before counting ownership: treating an unknown enable as false would quietly erase a potential driver. In a testbench, run the check in a sampling phase consistent with the receiver, and drive the inputs before that phase. Run the equal-data contention case with both inputs set to one as a regression. A test that simply compares the received value against one passes this case; the ownership assertion must fail. If the design permits a handover gap, test it at both an idle and an active sample to confirm that the transfer indicator, not just the bus bits, controls the observation.
